    {
      "@id": "dict:concept/fractured-locus",
      "@type": "Type",
      "identified_by": [
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "Fractured Locus",
          "language": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "균열된 장소",
          "language": "ko"
        }
      ],
      "dictrel:entityType": "Concept",
      "dictrel:documentedIn": [
        "dictee"
      ],
      "dictrel:mappingStatus": "noExternalAuthority",
      "referred_to_by": [
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "Term developed by María Lugones (2010) to describe the tension between colonial designation and active subjectivity within communal memory. Refers to the space of agency available to those positioned outside dominant social categories.",
          "classified_as": "description"
        },
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "'Her identity... is exchangeable with any other heroine in history, their names, dates, actions which require not definition in their deviation to generosity and self-sacrifice.' (p.30). Lugones (2010): tension between colonial designation as 'non-human' and active subjectivity within communal memory. NOT erasure of individuality but refusal of singular hero narrative.",
          "classified_as": "scope_note"
        }
      ],
      "dict:evidenceType": "Direct Term",
      "dict:chapter": [
        "CLIO",
        "ERATO"
      ],
      "dict:pageReference": "p.30",
      "skos:broader": [
        {
          "id": "dict:concept/postcolonial-feminism",
          "dictrel:documentedIn": [
            "dictee"
          ],
          "dict:note": "Fractured Locus is a sub-concept within the postcolonial feminist framework."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "dict:concept/cyclical-time",
      "@type": "Type",
      "identified_by": [
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "Cyclical time",
          "language": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "순환적 시간",
          "language": "ko"
        }
      ],
      "dictrel:entityType": "Concept",
      "dictrel:documentedIn": [
        "dictee"
      ],
      "dictrel:mappingStatus": "noExternalAuthority",
      "referred_to_by": [
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "A non-linear conception of time in which historical events recur as structural repetitions rather than sequential causes and effects. Draws on Lugones (2010). Applied in this dataset to the recurring pattern of colonial violence across different historical moments.",
          "classified_as": "description"
        },
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "'Dead time. Hollow depression interred... resistant to memory. Waits.' (p.123). Time buried underground, requiring excavation by diseuse's invocation. Connected to Persephone in underworld. 'Until.' — permanent incompletion. Theoretical basis: Lugones (2010) [primary]; Kusch (tr.2010, orig.1978) via Lugones — Andean context, NOT directly applied to Korean text.",
          "classified_as": "scope_note"
        }
      ],
      "dict:evidenceType": "Modeling",
      "dict:chapter": [
        "ELITERE",
        "THALIA"
      ],
      "dict:pageReference": "p.123"
    },
    {
      "@id": "dict:concept/ungendering",
      "@type": "Type",
      "identified_by": [
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "Ungendering",
          "language": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "탈성별화",
          "language": "ko"
        }
      ],
      "dictrel:entityType": "Concept",
      "dictrel:documentedIn": [
        "dictee"
      ],
      "dictrel:mappingStatus": "noExternalAuthority",
      "referred_to_by": [
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "Term developed by Hortense Spillers (1987) to describe the exclusion of enslaved and colonized women from normative gender categories entirely. Distinct from gender nonconformity: ungendering refers to a structural violence rather than a chosen identity position.",
          "classified_as": "description"
        },
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "Spillers (1987): exclusion from gender categories entirely under colonial/slave violence. Enslaved women as 'female but not woman' — reduced to 'zero degree of social conceptualisation.' Applied to URANIA chapter: 'Sang. Encre.' (Blood. Ink.) — Cha's body treated as flesh (살덩어리) under medical/colonial gaze, not as gendered subject. Blood extraction (forced, external) → transformation into ink (self-determined) marks transition from ungendering to re-gendering.",
          "classified_as": "scope_note"
        }
      ],
      "dict:evidenceType": "Direct Term",
      "dict:chapter": [
        "URANIA"
      ],
      "dict:pageReference": "pp.64-65",
      "skos:broader": [
        {
          "id": "dict:concept/postcolonial-feminism",
          "dictrel:documentedIn": [
            "dictee"
          ],
          "dict:note": "Ungendering is a sub-concept within the postcolonial feminist framework."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "dict:concept/diasporic-experience",
      "@type": "Type",
      "identified_by": [
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "Diasporic experience",
          "language": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "디아스포라 경험",
          "language": "ko"
        }
      ],
      "dictrel:entityType": "Concept",
      "dictrel:documentedIn": [
        "dictee"
      ],
      "dictrel:mappingStatus": "noExternalAuthority",
      "skos:closeMatch": [
        {
          "id": "https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300417672"
        }
      ],
      "referred_to_by": [
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "The condition of displacement and longing for return that cannot be fully realized. Draws primarily on Avtar Brah's concept of 'homing desire' (1996), which distinguishes between the mythologized idea of home and the possibility of literal return. Applied to multi-generational displacement across Manchuria, Korea, and the United States.",
          "classified_as": "description"
        },
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "Brah, Cartographies of Diaspora (1996) [primary]: 'homing desire' — diaspora space as mythologized, not literal, return. Cha returns to Korea 1980 but witnesses Gwangju; Bari returns with medicine but cure unconfirmed. 'Until.' as grammar of unfinished waiting. Diaspora as structural incompletion. Clifford, 'Diasporas' (1994) [secondary — heritage/boundary frame, distinct from Brah]. Multi-displacement: Manchuria → Korea → Hawaii → US mainland.",
          "classified_as": "scope_note"
        }
      ],
      "dict:evidenceType": "Synthesis",
      "dict:chapter": [
        "ELITERE",
        "POLYMNIA"
      ],
      "dict:pageReference": "pp.123, 133, 175"
    },
    {
      "@id": "dict:concept/untranslatability",
      "@type": "Type",
      "identified_by": [
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "Untranslatability",
          "language": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "Name",
          "content": "번역불가능성",
          "language": "ko"
        }
      ],
      "dictrel:entityType": "Concept",
      "dictrel:documentedIn": [
        "dictee"
      ],
      "dictrel:mappingStatus": "noExternalAuthority",
      "referred_to_by": [
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "The impossibility of full semantic transfer between languages. Draws on Spivak's Death of a Discipline (2003) and Apter's The Translation Zone (2006). In this dataset, refers to the deliberate use of multilingual ambiguity — including French homophones and verb tense distinctions — as a strategy of resistance in Cha's text.",
          "classified_as": "description"
        },
        {
          "type": "LinguisticObject",
          "content": "Spivak, Death of a Discipline (2003); Apter, The Translation Zone (2006). Impossibility of full semantic transfer between languages. Deliberate mistranslation, broken grammar, and silence as strategies exposing this impossibility. In Dictée: cygnes/signes (homophones in French, untranslatable to English); J'écoutais (imparfait, active listening) vs I heard (simple past, passive receipt) — each language preserves an untranslatable political remainder. The text refuses easy comprehension by Western readers.",
          "classified_as": "scope_note"
        }
      ],
      "dict:evidenceType": "Direct Term",
      "dict:chapter": [
        "CALLIOPE",
        "URANIA",
        "ERATO"
      ]
    },
    {
